## Appending a helper

The simplest, safest pattern is "append after end of file":

- Run `file_editor view` to see the file. Note the highest line number it shows; call this `L_view`.
- Call `fix_at_line` with `start_line` = `end_line` = `L_view + 1`, `old_block` = `""`, `new_block` = `<helper source>`.
- The executor recognises this exact shape (start == end == total_lines + 1, empty `old_block`) as a deliberate "append after EOF" and appends without needing a content anchor.

Why `L_view + 1` and not `L_view`: `file_editor view` may display one trailing line beyond the file's actual content (a UI affordance for "you can append here"). Targeting `L_view + 1` lands cleanly past the file regardless of which convention the view uses.

After each append the file grows; re-`view` (or just add the helper's line count to your local `L_view`) before the next append.

Setting `start_line`/`end_line` to a line that contains existing function code — e.g. "the line immediately after the original function" or any line within an existing helper — overwrites that line. Don't do that. The append-at-EOF pattern is the safe default.

If for some reason you must anchor on existing content (rare), use `start_line` = `end_line` = the line number of the LAST line that contains real code, set `old_block` = that line's exact text, set `new_block` = `<old_block>` + `\n\n` + `<helper source>`.

## Applying the fix

To edit the file, emit exactly one `fix_at_line` tool call per edit. The message carrying that call MUST contain only the call.

