### Choosing `old_block`

For a multi-line replacement (anything bigger than one or two lines — e.g. replacing the original function with its simplified version), use the **empty `old_block`** form:

- `start_line` = first line to replace (inclusive, 1-indexed)
- `end_line`   = last line to replace (inclusive, 1-indexed)
- `old_block`  = `""`  (empty string — the executor reads "trust my line range; replace this whole window without byte-verifying the existing content")
- `new_block`  = the new content for those lines

This is the recommended form for refactor edits. Do NOT try to copy the original 100+ line function body into `old_block` as a sanity check — long multi-line strings get mangled in tool calls and the byte-match will fail. The line range itself is the anchor; you have just `view`ed the file and you trust your numbers.

For a small inline edit (renaming a single identifier on one line, swapping a literal value, etc.) you may instead pass `old_block` as a unique substring of the line(s) — the executor will find-and-replace within the anchored range.

### Invalidation rules

Each of the following invalidates the call (the file stays unedited):

- A literal-syntax call written as text, e.g. `fix_at_line(start_line=..., ...)`.
- A code block holding the call or its arguments.
- A JSON object representing the call.
- Prose describing the call or any of its arguments.

If any is present, discard the message and emit only the tool call.

## Refactoring rules — these are absolute

- Bring cognitive complexity strictly below 15.
- Extract one or more helper functions; the simplified original function MUST call them.
- Match the helper's signature style to the original function:
  - Original `def f(x):` (free function or `@staticmethod` on class) → helpers `def _helper(x):`, called `_helper(x)` (or `Cls._helper(x)` if static on a class)
  - Original `def f(self, x):` (instance method) → helpers `def _helper(self, x):`, called `self._helper(x)`
